WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.135
But I feel like
the common thread

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:05.004
with maybe marriage
and sketch comedy is commitment.

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:07.140
Like, you got to have
commitment for both, right?

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.508
You do.
You really do. Yeah.

00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:10.076
I mean, isn't that the key?

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.978
I mean, I've never been
a sketch player.

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.314
You are the genius--

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:16.649
to my view, genius
sketch player of all time.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:17.850
I mean, you've-- Seriously.

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:20.019
-(AUDIENCE APPLAUDS)
-I'm not just saying that
'cause you're here.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.287
I've always believed that.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.923
Even when you insulted me,
I believed that.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.192
You're a genius at what you do.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.327
But it is about commitment,
right?

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:32.398
You always commit one billion
percent to the character.

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:35.568
There's never any doubt
you're in it all the way.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.771
Yeah. Yeah, I--
To me, it's always--

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:41.140
To me, sketch performing
is just acting.

00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:43.943
I try to act as seriously
within the sketch

00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:45.411
as Meryl Streep would

00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:46.512
-in any drama.
-BILL MAHER: Right.

00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:47.980
You know, because the thing
is that what--

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.817
you want to be a mile deep
in the commitment.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.419
Not only-- Not only
of you to the character,

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.287
but the character
to the situation

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:56.856
-they find themselves in.
-MAHER: Right.

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:58.758
And that's always been
important to me is that

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:01.293
I wanted to make sure
that I'm playing it

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:03.796
as deep as possible,
so that--

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.965
'Cause it's not about me
winking or laughing,

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:07.600
it's about you guys laughing.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.468
And it's about giving
as much commitment to the--

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:10.870
to the project as possible.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.773
And this, I feel like
<i> Key &amp; Peele...</i>

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:17.276
(APPLAUSE)

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.679
I mean, I'm watching
<i> Key &amp; Peele</i> now again,

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.747
-because it's--
they just put it on Netflix.
-KEEGAN-MICHAEL KEY: Right.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:23.749
I mean, I always watched them
when they've come
the first time.

00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:25.084
Probably watched it twice.

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.053
So probably, I'm watching it
the third time now.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.021
Stands the test of time,

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:31.557
which is not something you can
say about a lot of comedy.

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.259
I mean, I--
going through your book,

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:36.196
I mean, you give, you know,
a lot of props to people.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:39.732
But I must say, like when
you watch old shows, they--

00:01:39.865 --> 00:01:41.266
Humor is not like music.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.870
People want to hear the music
from 50 years ago.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.805
You watch a sketch
from 50 years ago...

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:46.839
KEY: Doesn't stand up.

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:49.208
-It really-- It rarely does.
-KEY: Rarely stands up.

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:50.576
-Your shit does.
-KEY: Yeah.

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:52.645
It's still exactly--
It really does.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.513
-(APPLAUSE)
-It's amazing.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:56.716
But like, things change.

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:58.517
Like, I feel like you guys,
you know,

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.420
you give great props
to Carol Burnett.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:02.388
-KEY: Mm-hmm.
-Which, right in this building.

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:03.689
-KEY: Yeah.
-Where that show was done.

00:02:03.822 --> 00:02:04.824
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:07.159
<i> Saturday Night Live</i>
and<i> Carol Burnett</i>

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:09.529
kind of overlapped
as sketch shows, right?

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:10.997
But very different.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:14.133
<i>Carol Burnett,</i> they were like
always cracking each other up.

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.669
Right? Harvey Korman
and Tim Conway-- (LAUGHS)

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.771
They couldn't get through
the sketch without laughing

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.072
-at what they were doing.
-KEY: Yeah.

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:22.575
<i>Saturday Night Live</i> was like,
"That's fucking corny."

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:23.876
-(CHUCKLING)
-KEY: That's right.

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:26.646
"We don't do that anymore."
And they never did.

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:28.847
And that just shows
things change.

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:30.216
So, when you watch
a<i> Carol Burnett</i> sketch,

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.619
it does look a little museum-y.

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:34.486
KEY: Hm. Interesting.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.021
-Interesting. (LAUGHS)
-I think--

00:02:36.121 --> 00:02:37.891
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:39.558
KEY: I can feel--

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:42.629
I can feel Harvey Korman's ghost
over my shoulder.

